message: "If you use this software or the associated data in your work, please cite both the paper and the software archive as described below."

title: "Thermal Spray ROM Comparison — 1D Flattening Analysis"
abstract: >-
  Reduced-order modeling (ROM) framework for particle flattening dynamics in
  thermal spray, benchmarked against Nishioka and Fukumoto's (2000)
  experimental Re–ξ data for nickel particles (40, 80, and 120 µm).
  The framework constructs a full-order model from discrete Re–ξ measurements,
  fits Schiller–Naumann drag and power-law correlations, and applies four ROM
  methods — Time-Derivative Decomposition (TD), Jacobian-Projection (JK-P),
  Jacobian-Galerkin with RK4 integrator (JK-G_RK4), and Jacobian-Galerkin with
  exponential integrator (JK-G_Exp). Performance is evaluated with energy
  retention, peak error, phase error, and FRF H₂ error, and compared against
  the theoretical Jones, Madejski, and Mostaghimi flattening models.

